{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent low bias: forecasts are systematically below realized prices for most of the first ~20–25 days (often by ~1–3 EUR), especially during rising regimes.",
    "Underestimates trend strength/volatility: when the market breaks higher, the model follows but with insufficient amplitude (misses peaks), implying a damped response to momentum.",
    "Late-horizon overshoot/failed mean reversion: around days ~25–30 the model often projects continued strength and overshoots, while realized prices flatten or revert lower (model too trend-following late).",
    "Turning-point lag: after local highs, the model is slow to price in pullbacks; it keeps levels elevated for several days even as outcomes roll over.",
    "Horizon-dependent correction: apply an upward level shift for short/mid horizons (≈day 1–20), but reduce/clip forecasts and add mean-reversion downward adjustment for the tail (≈day 25–30), especially after strong run-ups."
  ]
}
